## Overview

This document outlines the best practices, conventions, and standards for the mp-core visualization layer. The project uses a **dual dashboard strategy**: React for full-featured, complex dashboards and Streamlit for quick, lightweight dashboards.

## Technology Stack

### React Dashboards (Full-featured)
- **React 18+**: Modern React with functional components and hooks
- **React Router DOM**: Client-side routing and navigation
- **Axios**: HTTP client for API communication with Flask backend
- **Charting**: Recharts, Chart.js, or Plotly.js for data visualization

### Streamlit Dashboards (Quick/Lightweight)
- **Streamlit**: Python-native dashboard framework
- **Plotly**: Interactive charts via `st.plotly_chart`
- **Pandas**: Data manipulation and display via `st.dataframe`
- **Altair**: Declarative statistical visualizations (optional)

### Shared Backend
- **Flask REST API**: Both React and Streamlit consume the same Flask API endpoints
- **Direct DB access**: Streamlit apps may also read directly from PostgreSQL for simplicity

## Dual Dashboard Strategy

| Criteria | React | Streamlit |
|---|---|---|
| **Use when** | Complex interactions, real-time updates, multi-page apps | Quick data exploration, internal tools, prototyping |
| **Examples** | Modem grid visualization, pool management UI | Scan result viewer, hardware status report, CSV analyzer |
| **Interactivity** | Full (drag-drop, modals, forms, websockets) | Widgets (sliders, selectors, buttons) |
| **Deployment** | Built static files served by Flask or nginx | `streamlit run app.py` on server |
| **State** | React hooks, context | `st.session_state` |
| **Update frequency** | Real-time / polling | On-demand / auto-rerun |

